In Terraform, Data Sources are used to read or fetch information about existing resources (that were not created by the current Terraform code).

Think of it like this for a DevOps Engineer:

resource → Used to create/manage new infrastructure

data → Used to fetch information about existing infrastructure

So data sources = read-only lookup of existing resources.

1. Simple Definition (DevOps Beginner)
A Terraform Data Source lets you get details of an existing resource from a cloud provider (like AWS) so that you can use those details in your Terraform configuration.

Example things you may fetch:

Existing VPC

Existing AMI

Existing Security Group

Existing Subnets

Existing Route Tables

Existing IAM Roles

2. Syntax of Data Source
data "<PROVIDER>_<RESOURCE_TYPE>" "<NAME>" {
  argument = value
}
Example:

data "aws_vpc" "main" {
  id = "vpc-123456"
}
Here:

Part	Meaning
data	Terraform keyword for reading existing resource
aws_vpc	AWS VPC data source
main	local name used inside terraform
id	filter to find the VPC
3. Real DevOps Scenario (Very Common)
Scenario
In most companies:

Network team creates VPC

DevOps team deploys applications inside that VPC

So DevOps should NOT create the VPC again.

Instead they fetch existing VPC using data source.

4. Real AWS Example (Production Style)
Step 1 — Get Existing VPC
data "aws_vpc" "prod_vpc" {
  filter {
    name   = "tag:Name"
    values = ["production-vpc"]
  }
}
This will fetch the existing VPC created earlier.

Step 2 — Get Subnets from that VPC
data "aws_subnets" "prod_subnets" {
  filter {
    name   = "vpc-id"
    values = [data.aws_vpc.prod_vpc.id]
  }
}
Notice this:

data.aws_vpc.prod_vpc.id
means:

Take VPC ID from the fetched VPC
Step 3 — Launch EC2 inside that VPC
resource "aws_instance" "app_server" {

  ami           = "ami-0abcdef1234567890"
  instance_type = "t3.micro"

  subnet_id = data.aws_subnets.prod_subnets.ids[0]

  tags = {
    Name = "app-server"
  }

}
5. What is Happening Here
Existing VPC  →  Terraform fetches it (data source)
Existing Subnets → Terraform fetches them
Then
Create EC2 inside those subnets
Architecture:

AWS (Already Exists)
     │
     │
     ├── VPC
     ├── Subnets
     └── Security Groups
           │
           │
     Terraform Data Sources
           │
           │
     Terraform Resources
           │
           │
       EC2 / EKS / RDS
6. Another Very Real DevOps Example (AMI Lookup)
Instead of hardcoding AMI ID (bad practice), DevOps fetches latest AMI.

data "aws_ami" "amazon_linux" {

  most_recent = true

  owners = ["amazon"]

  filter {
    name   = "name"
    values = ["amzn2-ami-hvm-*"]
  }
}
Now use it:

resource "aws_instance" "server" {

  ami           = data.aws_ami.amazon_linux.id
  instance_type = "t3.micro"

}
Now Terraform will automatically use latest AMI.

