data.terraform_remote_state.network.outputs.vpc_id
5. What You CANNOT Access
This will NOT work:

data.terraform_remote_state.network.aws_vpc.main.id
Terraform will give an error because resources are not exposed.

Only this works:

data.terraform_remote_state.network.outputs.vpc_id
6. Multiple Outputs Example
Network project:

output "vpc_id" {
  value = aws_vpc.main.id
}

output "public_subnets" {
  value = aws_subnet.public[*].id
}

output "private_subnets" {
  value = aws_subnet.private[*].id
}
Another Terraform project can read them:

data.terraform_remote_state.network.outputs.vpc_id

data.terraform_remote_state.network.outputs.public_subnets

data.terraform_remote_state.network.outputs.private_subnets
7. Why Terraform Uses This Design
Three important reasons:

1️⃣ Security
State files may contain sensitive data.

2️⃣ Modular infrastructure
Different teams expose only what others need.

3️⃣ Stable interface
Outputs act like an API between Terraform projects.

8. Real Production DevOps Architecture
Typical infrastructure separation:

network-terraform
   outputs:
      vpc_id
      subnets

eks-terraform
   reads remote state
   uses vpc_id + subnets

app-terraform
   reads same remote state
So outputs become the shared contract between projects.

